= The Steel Strike

The steelworkers are on national strike for the first time since 1926. British Steel offered 2% while losing hundreds of millions a year; the Iron and Steel Trades Confederation wants 20%. It is the first big test of the government's refusal to bail out nationalised industries.

- @hold_firm: Hold firm: British Steel must settle within its cash limits.
- @settle: Find the money to settle it.

@hold_firm
on-arrival: union_power -= 6; budget -= 1; economic_growth -= 0.3; dry_dissent -= 4; wet_dissent += 3; c2_con += 1*(1-dissent) if union_backlash >= 60

After thirteen weeks the strikers settle for about 16%, paid for through productivity deals and closures. Neither side wins outright, but the government didn't write a cheque.

@settle
on-arrival: budget -= 2; union_power += 5; strike_wave -= 2; dry_dissent += 6; wet_dissent -= 3; economic_liberalism -= 2

The Treasury finds the money and the strike ends. The unions conclude that this government can be pushed.
